Let $d(v)$ be the shortest distance from vertex 1 to vertex $v$ in the graph. The condition is that $|\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}| = |\{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}|$. Since there are $N$ vertices in total, this implies that exactly $N/2$ vertices have even distance and $N/2$ vertices have odd distance. Note that $d(1) = 0$, which is even. So vertex 1 is always in the "even" set.

Let $S_{even} = \{v \mid d(v) \text{ is even}\}$ and $S_{odd} = \{v \mid d(v) \text{ is odd}\}$. We require $|S_{even}| = |S_{odd}| = N/2$.
Since $1 \in S_{even}$, we need to choose $N/2 - 1$ other vertices to be in $S_{even}$ from the remaining $N-1$ vertices. Let $k = N/2$. We choose a subset $A \subset \{2, \dots, N\}$ of size $k-1$. Let $S_{even} = \{1\} \cup A$ and $S_{odd} = \{2, \dots, N\} \setminus A$.
For a fixed partition of vertices into $S_{even}$ and $S_{odd}$, we need to count the number of connected graphs where the bipartition of the BFS layers (or rather, the parity of distances) matches this partition.
