Specifically, if $u \in S_{even}$ and $v \in S_{odd}$, then any path between $u$ and $v$ must have odd length. If $u, v \in S_{even}$, any path must have even length. If $u, v \in S_{odd}$, any path must have odd length.
Actually, the condition "shortest distance from 1 is even/odd" implies that in the graph, if we consider the bipartite graph formed by edges $(u, v)$ where $d(u) + d(v)$ is odd, these edges connect $S_{even}$ and $S_{odd}$.
However, the condition is stronger. It's not just that the graph is bipartite with parts $S_{even}$ and $S_{odd}$. It's that the shortest path distance from 1 to any node in $S_{even}$ is even, and to any node in $S_{odd}$ is odd.
